==Trivia==
{{LongSection|text=
===General===
*It's implied that Wong's "[[wikipedia:Sifu|Sifu]]" is [[Jackie Peng]], who loves kung-fu, hides under a [[The Burgle Cats/Traps|manhole]] and has a similar coloration to Wong in this game.
*Vars shares a name with the [[:Category:Uber Rare Cats|Uber Rare Cat]] [[Vars (Uber Rare Cat)|Vars]] from [[The Battle Cats]], but this was most likely unintentional, as their names are different in Japanese: The Burgle Cats Vars is バース ''Bāsu'', while The Battle Cats Vars is バルス ''Barusu''.
*Kashiwa's description is missing the apostrophe in "Let's".
*Musk was originally called "The Mask", which was likely a mistranslation.

===References===
*Sean may be an homage to [[PONOS Corporation|PONOS]] translator Sean Koble, as both are bilingual.
*Manju's stage name, Sno-Brite, is a pun on "[[wikipedia:Snow White|Snow White]]".
*Shigeru being a veteran of the video game industry for over 30 years is a reference to [[wikipedia:Shigeru Miyamoto|Shigeru Miyamoto]], while his brown fur could allude to singer and actor [[wikipedia:Shigeru Matsuzaki|Shigeru Matsuzaki]], also known as Shigeru Brown.
*Gretel's name and distaste for sweet things reference the fairy tale [[wikipedia:Hansel and Gretel|Hansel and Gretel]], in which two children find and start to eat a gingerbread house, only to realize that it was a trap set up by a cannibalistic witch.
*Dorakichi's name and description both reference ''[[wikipedia:Doraemon|Doraemon]]''.
*Savannah's name and Japanese description, which states that her voice is so loud it reaches as far as Brazil, reference the comedy duo [[wikipedia:Savanna (owarai)|Savanna]], consisting of Shigeo Takahashi and Masumi Yagi, and one of the latter's trademark gags, "[https://youtu.be/hjx_Qmf0-kY Brazil no hito kikoemasuka?]" (ブラジルの人聞こえますか？).
*Poppy's name, description, color scheme and triangle on her forehead reference [[wikipedia:ja:ピーポくん|Pīpo-kun]], the mouse-like mascot of the Tokyo Metropolitan Police Department.
*"Iron Cat Chef" in Alton's description is a reference to the cooking show ''[[wikipedia:Iron Chef|Iron Chef]]''.
*Tom's name, design and love of chases reference Tom from ''[[wikipedia:Tom and Jerry|Tom and Jerry]]''.
*T-Bone's facial markings and color scheme are identical to those of Rascal from the animated series ''[[wikipedia:Rascal the Raccoon|Rascal the Raccoon]]''. T-Bone's Japanese name, Arai, comes from ''araiguma'', the Japanese word for raccoon.
*Hamlet is essentially [[wikia:hamtaro:Hamtaro|Hamtaro]] in cat form.
*Reynard's Japanese name, Inari, refers to [[wikipedia:Inari Ōkami|Inari Ōkami]], the Japanese ''[[wikipedia:Kami|kami]]'' of foxes, fertility, rice, agriculture, industry and worldly success. It was localized to Reynard in order to reference a different fox-related figure, [[wikipedia:Reynard the Fox|Reynard the Fox]].
*Koji's Japanese name, Kabirun, is taken from Kabirunrun, the minions of Baikinman in the picture books-turned-anime ''[[wikipedia:Anpanman|Anpanman]]''.
*Kaneda's color scheme, mouth, star on his forehead and attempts at "training to be a ninja in the Iga School style" but quitting after one week all reference the lazy dog [[wikia:ninjahattori:Shishimaru|Shishimaru]] from ''[[wikipedia:Ninja Hattori-kun|Ninja Hattori-kun]]'', while his Japanese name, Kanetetsu, is taken from [https://en.kanetetsu.com/ Kanetetsu Delica Foods], a food manufacturer and seafood processing company that sponsored ''Ninja Hattori-kun''.
*Hawk's description, fur colors and Japanese name (Aiū) reference the [[Felyne (Special Cat)|Felynes]] (Airū in Japanese) from the ''Monster Hunter'' series.
*Wyatt resembles [[Futenyan (Rare Cat)|Futenyan]], with his Japanese name, Waimo, alluding to the company Futenyan is the mascot of, [[Y! Mobile Collaboration Event|<u>Y! Mo</u>bile]].
*Moco's name, cooking skill and love for the smell of olive oil reference actor, chef and model [[wikipedia:Mocomichi Hayami|Mocomichi Hayami]], well known for having a similar passion for olive oil.
*Walker's [[:Category:Zombie Enemies|Zombie]]-like appearance and Japanese name (Walkin) reference ''[[wikipedia:The Walking Dead|The Walking Dead]]''.
*Daisy closely resembles the titular character of the manga ''[[wikipedia:Chimpui|Chimpui]]'' and has a similar Japanese name (Puipui).
*The show "Real Housecats" in Daisy's description is a reference to ''[[wikipedia:The Real Housewives|The Real Housewives]]''.
*Wilson's design and Japanese name (Watch) reference ''[[wikipedia:Yo-kai Watch|Yo-kai Watch]]'' and its mascot [[wikia:yokaiwatch:Jibanyan|Jibanyan]].
*Gordon bears more than a passing resemblance to [[wikipedia:Garfield|Garfield]].
*Corban's design is based on the Pokémon [[bulbapedia:Meowth (Pokémon)|Meowth]].
*Lucas's bizarre appearance is reminiscent of the anthropomorphic gopher [https://youtu.be/EAzKAwP1KBQ Gonta-kun] from the children's television show [[wikipedia:Dekirukana|''Dekirukana'']], while his preference for "tall, silent types" refers to Noppo-san from the same show. His Japanese name, Rukkana, also sounds similar to ''Dekirukana''.
*Blaze's Japanese name (Yamanzu), tanuki-like appearance and occupation as firefighter reference the Japanese folktale ''[[wikipedia:Kachi-kachi Yama|Kachi-kachi Yama]]'', which involves a mischievous tanuki's back being burned when a rabbit sets fire to the kindling he was carrying. His color scheme and the "mileage points" in his description also reference the point service [[wikipedia:ja:Ponta|Ponta]], whose mascot (seen [https://point.recruit.co.jp/point/ here]) is an orange-and-white tanuki.
*Jasper's Japanese name (Gonbei), fox-like appearance, mischievous nature and knack for secretly giving people chestnuts (and mushrooms, according to his Japanese description) reference the children's story ''[[wikipedia:Gon, the Little Fox|Gon, the Little Fox]]''.
*Pistachio is based on the ''[[wikipedia:Bake-danuki|bake-danuki]]'', a shapeshifting tanuki ''[[wikipedia:Yōkai (folklore)|yōkai]]''.
*Alastair's Japanese name, Yamato, references two different things. With his black-fur, it refers to the the delivery company [https://www.kuronekoyamato.co.jp/en/ Yamato Transport], commonly known as ''Kuroneko'' ("Black Cat"). With his love of interstellar travel and building plastic spaceship models, it alludes to ''[[wikipedia:Space Battleship Yamato|Space Battleship Yamato]]''.
*Lemony's description seems to reference the overall plot of the anime ''[[wikipedia:Tamako Market|Tamako Market]]'', especially with their first name in Japanese, Mochizō (餅蔵), also being the first name of Tamako's childhood friend Mochizō Oji (大路 もち蔵).
*Trolley's design is based on Totoro from ''[[wikipedia:My Neighbor Totoro|My Neigbor Totoro]]''.
*Nora's design is based on [https://www.wikimoon.org/index.php/Luna Luna] from ''[[wikipedia:Sailor Moon|Sailor Moon]]''.
*Alice resembles the [[wikipedia:Cheshire Cat|Cheshire Cat]] from ''[[wikipedia:Alice's Adventures in Wonderland|Alice in Wonderland]]'', while the folk trio mentioned in her description refers to the Japanese folk trio [[wikipedia:ja:アリス (フォークグループ)|Alice]].
*Picarin is based on the Pokémon [[bulbapedia:Pikachu (Pokémon)|Pikachu]]. His moonlighting as a private eye references ''[[wikipedia:Detective Pikachu (video game)|Detective Pikachu]]'' in particular.
*Since he "works at an [[wikipedia:Tesla, Inc.|electric car maker]]," Musk is likely named after [[wikipedia:Elon Musk|Elon Musk]].
*Woody's Japanese name, Marumokkoru, references [[wikipedia:Marimokkori|Marimokkori]], a similarly green mascot for the Japanese island of [[wikipedia:Hokkaido|Hokkaido]].
*Lucky's design is based on [[wikipedia:Daruma doll|''Daruma'' dolls]].
*Artie's blue fur with purple spots and "monstrous complexion" reference Sully from ''[[wikipedia:Monsters, Inc.|Monsters, Inc.]]'' His Japanese name, Pikuchā, sounds like Pikusā, the Japanese pronunciation of [[wikipedia:Pixar|Pixar]].
*Noelle not only resembles [[wikia:dragonball:Frieza|Frieza]] from ''[[wikipedia:Dragon Ball|Dragon Ball]]'', but also rubs her back with a dry towel 530,000 times every day of winter according to her Japanese description, with 530,000 being the exact power level of Frieza's first form.
}}

